        "Innaloo was mostly built between the Second World War and 1970. Development accelerated during the war years and the original stock is single detached timber-framed homes, many of them weatherboard or asbestos cement on timber stumps, on lots that have since been steadily split for duplexes and units in brick-and-tile. The land was low-lying swamp and market garden before subdivision, with drainage channels dug to make it usable. That means high groundwater in winter, damp subfloors under the old cottages and a soil that stays moist long into summer. For subterranean termites, an old timber-framed house with a damp subfloor on a former swamp is about as good as it gets.",
        "Karrinyup Road, Scarborough Beach Road and Ellen Stirling Boulevard carry the traffic, and the Mitchell Freeway runs the eastern edge next to Stirling station. Westfield Innaloo, the Megacentre and the cinema precinct are a large commercial area with bins, loading docks and food outlets that keep German cockroach and rat populations topped up year-round. Birralee Park and Yuluma Park give open space, and the stormwater and sewer network draining the old flats is where American cockroaches live. We treat Innaloo homes for the full mix: subfloor moisture and termites in the cottages, cockroaches and rodents near the commercial precinct, and slab-edge termite entry in the newer units.",
