        "Only three buildings stood in Trigg in 1919; holiday and fishing shacks came in the 1920s and formal subdivision of 1,888 lots began in the late 1940s. Some of those shacks and post-war workers' cottages are still there, timber framed on limestone, alongside 1960s and 1970s brick-and-tile and a growing number of large two-storey rebuilds along the ocean side of West Coast Drive. The ground is Quindalup dune sand on the foreshore and Tamala limestone close to the surface almost everywhere else, which is why the suburb has its slope. Limestone means termite management systems are usually drilled and injected rather than trenched, and its moisture pockets keep termites active when the sand on top is dry.",
