        "Bedford was gazetted in 1937 but most of it was built after the Second World War, when the State Housing Commission bought large tracts and filled them with modest brick or fibro homes on timber-framed tile roofs. Development was essentially complete by the early 1970s, and the census still records the suburb as overwhelmingly detached houses, with a growing number of semi-detached rebuilds as the old quarter-acre blocks are split. The ground is Bassendean dune sand: pale grey, heavily leached and poor at holding nutrients, but with a shallow water table that keeps subfloors and garden beds damper than the yellow sand suburbs to the west. Damp sand under sixty-year-old timber framing is where we find most of Bedford's termite activity.",
